#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""ai$ha (ai shell hybrid alpha), run as `aisha` — a natural-language shell.

Type bash as usual, or say what you want in plain words:

    aisha ~/lab ❯ find files in this folder that have the measurement data
    → find · here and subfolders · files · name or content ~ "measur" (from "measurement"; "data" is generic)
    $ find . -path '*/.git' -prune -o -type f \\( -iname '*measur*' -o -exec grep -IqiF -e measur {} \\; \\) -print

Each line goes through these stages, in order, and stops at the first that
handles it:

  1. Shell input. A command on PATH with flags, paths, pipes or redirects
     runs in bash unchanged. `!` forces this stage.
  2. Memory. A phrase whose LLM-written command you approved before is
     replayed without asking the model again.
  3. Rules. A deterministic parser pulls slots out of the words (action,
     where, file types, size, age, name and content keywords, sort, limit)
     and builds the command from them. No model, instant, and it shows its
     reasoning.
  4. LLM. Only when the rules are not confident, a small local model is
     asked, with the parser's partial understanding attached as hints. Any
     OpenAI-compatible server works (Ollama, llama.cpp, LM Studio). Its
     command is always shown and confirmed before it runs. `?` forces this
     stage.

Commands that delete or overwrite data always ask first.

Standard library only. Python 3.9+.
"""

